{"step_index":196,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-30T21:59:41Z","thinking":"Examining the specified code section to understand the current implementation of the checkout workflow initialization. The initial focus is on how the close button and backdrop are handled, specifically the event listener associated with the close button. Further investigation is needed into the modal's reset logic and any associated dialogs that may require updates.\n\n\nInvestigating the conditional logic tied to the delivery mode switcher, focusing on how different UI elements are shown or hidden based on the selected mode. The code appears to manage the visibility of address input fields and map elements, likely controlling the user's data entry based on their delivery preference (delivery vs. other options). This implementation suggests a dynamic UI update based on user interaction with the delivery mode selection.\n\n\nThe delivery mode switcher's logic dictates UI element visibility. Elements associated with location input or map display are hidden when the mode is not 'delivery'. Conversely, a specific element is shown when the delivery mode is set to 'dinein'. It's essential to ensure UI responsiveness. The geolocation trigger attempts to retrieve the user's location via the browser's geolocation API. If the browser lacks this feature, an error message is displayed.\n\n\nGPS location retrieval uses the browser's geolocation API, updating customer location data upon success and showing an error message on failure. Payment method selection dynamically highlights the chosen payment option by updating CSS classes. Input validation removes error indicators on user input for various form fields.\n\nFurther analysis reveals the \"Done\" button in the WhatsApp dialog resets the checkout modal, clears the cart, and resets form and button states. Form submission is handled, targeting the `handleOrder` function after a conditional check.\n\n\nInitial assessment involves validating the cart and customer contact information before order processing. The function checks for an empty cart using a snapshot and displays an error if it's empty. Customer data validation appears next.\n\nThe code proceeds to gather customer data from various form fields. Each field's validity is evaluated individually; if any field is invalid, an error indicator is shown.  The `paymentMethod` is extracted from radio buttons; the default is set to \"Cash on Delivery\" if none are selected.\n\n\nCustomer input validation occurs next, specifically for name and phone number, with error states applied to invalid fields. Phone number validation includes a check for Indian mobile number format.\n\nAddress fields are validated conditionally, only if delivery mode is selected. The process includes distance checks and provides an error toast if the delivery location exceeds the permitted radius.\n\nFinally, the function displays an error toast and prevents proceeding if overall validation fails, keeping the user on the checkout page.\n\n\nChecking for the restaurant's WhatsApp number configuration, and aborting if it is missing or invalid, showing an error message. The code then disables the confirmation button and updates its text to indicate the message is being prepared.\n\nAn order reference is generated for the order. Subtotal and total amount are computed, the delivery fee is formatted according to delivery mode, and then constructing a dynamic WhatsApp message including order details.\n\n\nA detailed WhatsApp message is constructed, incorporating order details, customer information, and selected order type. Conditional logic determines what customer data to include based on the order type (delivery, takeaway, or dine-in).\n\nThe message incorporates the total order amount, and clearly indicates the chosen payment method, including instructions for order confirmation. Finally, the message is encoded for use within a WhatsApp link.\n\n\nOpening a new WhatsApp window with order details, with a fallback for popup blockers, triggering a link in the current window. A feedback dialog is displayed, showing the order ID and WhatsApp link within the modal. A notification indicates that WhatsApp is being opened, while re-enabling the confirmation button for reuse.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"C:\\Users\\Lenovo\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\brew-and-bloom\\script.js","EndLine":430,"StartLine":395,"toolAction":"Reading openCheckoutModal in script.js","toolSummary":"Inspect openCheckoutModal implementation"}}]}
